SELECT 'Agentic AI for Email Marketing Campaigns: Journeys That Run Themselves, Supervised', 'agentic-ai-email-marketing-campaign-automation', CONCAT('Writing better emails is one problem; running behaviour-triggered journeys for thousands of subscribers is another. Agentic AI handles the second ', CHAR(0xE2,0x80,0x94 USING utf8mb4), ' segmenting, generating personalised content, coordinating multi-step journeys, testing and reporting ', CHAR(0xE2,0x80,0x94 USING utf8mb4), ' with humans keeping consent, claims and the send button. Plus the CASL course, funded up to 70%.'),
  '<p><strong>Short version:</strong> There are two different email problems. The first is writing better emails, which generative AI already solves. The second is <em>operating</em> a live programme &mdash; thousands of subscribers at different journey stages, behaviour-triggered sends, tests running, deliverability to watch &mdash; which is a coordination problem no amount of better copy addresses. Agentic AI is aimed at the second: agents that analyse audience data, segment continuously, generate the personalised variant, trigger the next step from what someone actually did, and report on what is working. The boundary that keeps it safe is that consent, claims and the decision to send stay human. This article covers both halves. The hands-on version is the <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/casl-agentic-ai-for-email-marketing-campaign.html">CASL Agentic AI for Email Marketing Campaign</a> course (TGS-2026064473), funded up to 70%.</p> <h2>The operating problem, stated plainly</h2> <p>A mature email programme is not a series of campaigns; it is a set of overlapping journeys. Someone downloads a guide on Tuesday and enters a nurture track. Someone else abandons a registration form and should get a different message within a day. A customer from last year has stopped opening and needs a re-engagement attempt before removal. Each of those is a rule, a piece of content and a timing decision, and there are dozens of them running at once.</p> <p>Marketing automation platforms have offered this for years, and most businesses use perhaps a tenth of the capability &mdash; because building and maintaining the journeys, and writing content for every branch, is the bottleneck. Agentic AI attacks that bottleneck specifically, which is why the practical result is usually &ldquo;we finally use the platform we already paid for&rdquo;.</p> <h2>What the agents actually do</h2> <table> <thead><tr><th>Agent role</th><th>Responsibility</th><th>Human checkpoint</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td>Audience analyst</td><td>Reads subscriber behaviour, proposes and maintains segments, flags list-health problems</td><td>Approve segment definitions</td></tr> <tr><td>Content generator</td><td>Produces subject lines, body copy and calls to action per segment, on brand</td><td>Approve before first send; spot-check thereafter</td></tr> <tr><td>Journey coordinator</td><td>Maps the multi-step sequence, sets triggers and timing, handles branch logic</td><td>Approve the journey design</td></tr> <tr><td>Test runner</td><td>Generates variants, monitors results, reports the winner with confidence</td><td>Approve what gets promoted</td></tr> <tr><td>Performance analyst</td><td>Watches opens, clicks, conversions, unsubscribes, deliverability signals</td><td>Decide what changes next</td></tr> </tbody> </table> <p>The pattern is the one that recurs across every agentic marketing system: agents do continuous attention and assembly; humans approve the things that are hard to reverse. An email cannot be unsent, which makes send approval the single most important checkpoint in the list.</p> <h2>Triggers are where the conversions are</h2> <p>Behaviour-triggered email consistently outperforms scheduled broadcasts, because it arrives when the subscriber has just demonstrated interest. The triggers worth building first, in rough order of return:</p> <ol> <li><strong>Abandoned enquiry or registration</strong> &mdash; within 24 hours, acknowledging what they were looking at and answering the likeliest hesitation.</li> <li><strong>Content download</strong> &mdash; a follow-up that extends the topic they chose, not a generic newsletter.</li> <li><strong>Repeated visits to a specific page</strong> &mdash; interest without action usually means an unanswered objection.</li> <li><strong>Post-purchase</strong> &mdash; onboarding, then the next logical step. Existing customers are the cheapest revenue you have.</li> <li><strong>Disengagement</strong> &mdash; one honest re-engagement attempt, then removal. Keeping dead addresses hurts deliverability for everyone else on the list.</li> </ol> <p>An agent''s advantage here is that it can maintain all five without any of them being forgotten during a busy quarter, which is exactly how these programmes normally decay.</p> <h2>Personalisation that helps rather than unsettles</h2> <p>Agentic systems can personalise far beyond a first name, and the temptation is to demonstrate it. Resist that. The useful test: would the recipient be comfortable if they knew why they received this email? Referencing the guide they downloaded passes. Referencing how many times they visited the pricing page does not, even though the data supports it.</p> <p>The compliance frame in Singapore is the <a href="https://www.pdpc.gov.sg/overview-of-pdpa/the-legislation/personal-data-protection-act">PDPA</a> &mdash; consent for marketing messages, honouring unsubscribes, and handling personal data used for personalisation appropriately. The PDPC''s <a href="https://www.pdpc.gov.sg/guidelines-and-consultation">advisory guidelines</a> and its <a href="https://www.pdpc.gov.sg/help-and-resources/2020/01/model-ai-governance-framework">Model AI Governance Framework</a> are the references for using such data in AI-driven systems, and documenting your oversight is part of doing it properly.</p> <h2>A phased rollout that keeps trust intact</h2> <p>Email is the channel where an automation mistake is most visible and least reversible, so the sensible adoption path front-loads supervision and relaxes it only where the evidence supports doing so.</p> <table> <thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>What runs</th><th>Supervision level</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td>Weeks 1&ndash;2</td><td>Segmentation analysis and content drafting only; nothing sends automatically</td><td>Every email read before it goes</td></tr> <tr><td>Weeks 3&ndash;6</td><td>One approved journey live &mdash; usually the welcome sequence, because the content is stable</td><td>Weekly review of what sent and how it performed</td></tr> <tr><td>Weeks 7&ndash;12</td><td>Behaviour triggers added one at a time, each proven before the next</td><td>Spot-checks plus automatic pause thresholds</td></tr> <tr><td>Ongoing</td><td>Journeys run; agents propose changes and new tests</td><td>Approve changes; monthly full review</td></tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Notice that supervision never reaches zero. What changes is where it is applied: from reading every email at the start, to approving changes to a system whose behaviour you have already validated. Teams that skip the first two phases and switch everything on tend to discover a tone problem several thousand sends later, which is an expensive way to learn it.</p> <h2>Deliverability: the risk that scales with automation</h2> <p>Automation multiplies both good and bad practice. A programme sending more email to more segments more often can quietly damage its sender reputation, at which point everything underperforms regardless of copy quality. Watch four signals, and have the performance agent report them every week:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Bounce rate</strong> &mdash; rising hard bounces mean list hygiene has slipped.</li> <li><strong>Spam complaints</strong> &mdash; the most damaging metric; investigate any increase immediately.</li> <li><strong>Unsubscribe rate by journey</strong> &mdash; one badly targeted sequence can drag the whole programme.</li> <li><strong>Engagement trend among recent subscribers</strong> &mdash; falling engagement in the newest cohort predicts trouble before the aggregate shows it.</li> </ul> <p>Set thresholds that pause a journey automatically and alert a person. That is the one place where automatic action is clearly right: stopping.</p> <h2>Learn it hands-on, funded up to 70%</h2> <p>The <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/casl-agentic-ai-for-email-marketing-campaign.html">CASL &ndash; Agentic AI for Email Marketing Campaign</a> course (TGS-2026064473) is a two-day programme covering the operating side of email. Its three topics:</p> <ol> <li><strong>Implementing agentic AI email marketing campaigns</strong> &mdash; audience analysis, segmentation, personalised content generation, and multi-step campaign coordination with human oversight; subject lines, copy, calls to action and sequences aligned to brand guidelines.</li> <li><strong>AI-driven campaign performance and consumer insights analysis</strong> &mdash; open rates, click-through, conversions, unsubscribes and other indicators; identifying issues and improving targeting.</li> <li><strong>AI agent analysis of market trends and customer feedback</strong> &mdash; connecting agents with customer databases, eCommerce platforms and marketing automation tools, and using feedback to improve campaigns.</li> </ol> <p>The course also covers consent management, data privacy, content accuracy, deliverability and responsible AI use. Assessment is a written and a practical exam.</p> <table> <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Funding</th><th>What you pay (incl. 9% GST)</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td>Full course fee</td><td>&ndash;</td><td>S$800.00 before GST (S$72.00 GST)</td></tr> <tr><td>Singapore Citizens and PRs aged 21 and above</td><td>50% CASL funding</td><td><strong>S$472.00</strong></td></tr> <tr><td>Singapore Citizens aged 40 and above (MCES), or SME-sponsored SG/PR staff</td><td>70% CASL funding</td><td><strong>S$312.00</strong></td></tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Eligible Singaporeans can offset the nett fee with <a href="https://www.skillsfuture.gov.sg/">SkillsFuture Credit</a> or PSEA, and eligible Singapore-registered companies can tap SkillsFuture Enterprise Credit (SFEC); SME employers can also claim absentee payroll. The course runs over two full days (9:30am to 6:30pm), with physical classroom and synchronous online (Zoom) options. Fees are as published on the course page at the time of writing &mdash; the live page always carries the current fee and the next available dates.</p> <p>If you want the content-craft foundation first, <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-claude-cowork-for-email-marketing.html">WSQ Claude Cowork for Email Marketing</a> covers segmentation, sequences and testing in depth.</p> <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2> <div class="mmd-faq"> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">What can an AI agent do in email marketing that a template cannot?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Templates are static. Agents analyse behaviour continuously, maintain segments as people move between them, generate the right variant for each, coordinate multi-step journeys with branching, and report on what is working &mdash; without any of it being forgotten during a busy quarter.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Should AI agents send emails automatically?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Journeys can run automatically once approved, but the initial approval of content and journey design should be human, and anything making a claim needs review. Email cannot be unsent, which makes send approval the most important checkpoint in the system.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Which behaviour triggers are worth building first?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Abandoned enquiry or registration within 24 hours, content-download follow-ups, repeated visits to a key page, post-purchase onboarding, and a single honest re-engagement before removing lapsed subscribers. Those five cover most of the available return.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">How much personalisation is too much?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Use the comfort test: would the recipient be fine knowing why they got this email? Referencing what they downloaded passes; referencing how often they visited your pricing page does not, even though you have the data.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">How do I protect deliverability as volume grows?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Watch bounces, spam complaints, unsubscribe rate by journey, and engagement among your newest subscribers. Set thresholds that automatically pause a journey and alert a person &mdash; stopping is the one action worth automating outright.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">What is CASL funding and does SkillsFuture Credit apply?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>CASL courses carry the same subsidy structure as the WSQ range on this site &mdash; 50% for Singapore Citizens and PRs aged 21 and above, 70% for Singaporeans aged 40 and above or SME-sponsored staff &mdash; and SkillsFuture Credit, PSEA and SFEC can be applied to the nett fee for eligible learners and companies.</p></div> </details> </div> <h2>The bottom line</h2> <p>Better copy was never the whole problem. Operating a live email programme &mdash; segments that stay current, journeys triggered by real behaviour, tests that run to completion, deliverability watched every week &mdash; is a coordination job, and that is what agents are genuinely good at. Build the five triggers, keep personalisation on the comfortable side of the line, approve before sending, and automate only one action outright: stopping when a signal goes wrong.</p> <p><strong>Ready to run journeys instead of broadcasts?</strong> <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/casl-agentic-ai-for-email-marketing-campaign.html">Register for CASL &ndash; Agentic AI for Email Marketing Campaign</a> &mdash; 2 days, funded up to 70%, SkillsFuture Credit claimable.</p>',
  'Dr. Alfred Ang', 1, '2025-07-02', 'TGS-2026064473', 'TGS-2026064473',
  'Agentic AI for Email Marketing Campaigns | CASL Course Singapore',
  CONCAT('Run agentic email marketing ', CHAR(0xE2,0x80,0x94 USING utf8mb4), ' AI agents that segment audiences, generate personalised content, coordinate behaviour-triggered journeys, run A/B tests and report on performance, with human oversight on consent and claims. CASL course TGS-2026064473, funded up to 70%.'),
  'agentic ai email marketing, ai email automation, behaviour triggered email, lead nurturing ai, email journey automation, casl email marketing course, ai campaign optimisation, email deliverability ai, marketing automation singapore, ai agents email, personalised email ai, skillsfuture email course',
  112, 'manual-skip', 'manual-skip', NOW(), NOW()
FROM DUAL WHERE @is_sg = 1
